URL reference: https://unikraft.com/blog/the-mighty-microvm
DateReviewed: 2026-07-20
Description: Today's article tries to prove that the only way to truly isolate virtual workloads is with a (micro)VM. We start with the AI background - agents have to start fast, be strongly isolated, and you might be running lots of them - not hundreds, but millions. So, where do you run this? They mention that words like sandbox, container, microVM and isolate get treated interchangably. Now we dive in - they detail a normal Virtual Machine, a full guest OS where the boundry is hardware and policed by a hypervisor. Next is microVM, a minimal VM monitor being a layer that creates, runs and manages VMs - using the hardware boundry. We move on to container, isolated with kernel features like cgroups and seccomp, and the boundry is software. Then there is runtime isolation like JVM or isolate, where multiple tenants share one language runtime (V8 isolates, JVM, Wasm host), and the boundry is the runtime's own logic. Now defined, they move on to tackle things that mystify people -- the micro in microVM. In this case, they point out that it's the monitor that is small, not what runs inside. We now pivot to talking about the Trusted Computing Base, or what they say is the core thing one must trust - with VMs, it's about 250k lines of code, where kernel + container runtimes can push into 40M or more LoC. This is a solid arguement. They then list a bunch of CVEs, but fail to explain how LoC counts affect these; it's just implied. I like the overall message, but some of the evidence is a bit weaker then they claim. Still, a decent read.
BottomLine: Good piece about using microVMs over other multi-tenant tech
