URL reference: https://www.ajeetraina.com/securing-docker-sandboxes-a-quick-look-at-1password-credential-injection/
DateReviewed: 2026-07-21
Description: Our article today tackles the issue around where should credentials like keys and passwords live with a Docker container. We start with a diagram, so starting strong, before delving into the problem - credentials shouldn't live in plaintext in the container, but where can they live? Previously we often used .env files, but when running things like AI agents, they have direct access, and by design these files are plaintext. With another diagram, we see how things work - rather than local processes ever seeing the creds, a placeholder sits where the auth should be; and when it is sent for verification, it is magically swapped for the actual tokens needed to do the auth. They further explain that the secret never touches the container itself - by leveraging docker sandboxes, the container doesn't need the creds, and they are injected in the network as they leave the container - in this case, the creds live in your host. We now dive into a tutorial to set this up - for this exercise, we're going to use 1Password as the vault. They go through some of the nuance, and explain how to set up the initial auth, then how to reference your secrets, and finally the process to reference them, including how to get the sensitive part. Then they walk through the injection part, showing three ways to do so. Finally, they do an end to end walkthrough. They also include a handy troubleshooting guide. Recommended.
BottomLine: Good article on moving credentials out of your containers
