Description: Today's article dives into Linux kernel module rootkits, and their impact on security. They start with a first hand account - everything looks good in system tools and monitoring dashboards, but there are wild CPU spikes and strange encrypted packets to an unknown IP. They dive a bit into how a kernel module works - being privledged, essentially a trusted part of the kernel. As such, they can change system calls, and present a false picture of the system, obscuring malicious processes. They point out that security tools aren't generally able to detect this, as it happens at the kernel level -- so there isn't much for them to detect. They then pivot to VoidLink, a modern example of LKM rootkit deployment. It's not a single malware, but a suite of tools for malware authors to help hide their compromised hosts. It's adaptive, scanning the env and loading the tools needed for that exact scenario. They then go on to explain some mitigation against this, and how to be vigilant against this happening to you. A good read.
BottomLine: Strong piece on modern kernel module rootkit techniques
