URL reference: https://twitter.com/RafterSecurity/status/2077824339915964477
DateReviewed: 2026-07-25
Description: This article tackles June of 2026 - the month that AI toolchains came under attack. After a few high-profile issues, our article will tackle a few of them and try to pull some common threads from them. They start with TrustFall in early May, where a setting allowing MCP actions from a "trusted" folder allows spawning sessions with user privs. In early June, Miasma worm exploits npm and CI processes, after a compromised commiter account is leveraged to distribute bad images. Another campaign used a similar tactic - running a script when files are simply loaded into the IDE. Our next pivot is Hades, at the end of the first week in June, which targeted Python packages, and included a credential stealer, all run by just launching the interpreter. In the middle of June, Agentjacking was released - a POC which used error reporting to craft what looked like remediation guidance, which was then executed on other clients. At the same time, Mastra was compromised by an NPM vuln, leading to 140 packages compromised in just under 90 minutes. To end the month, another IDE attack on Amazon Q this time allowed MCP servers to be launched by dropping a file in the right directory, which gets full env access. Our author identifies the proper gate as the pull request - where code gets added to the repo, as the spot to do careful review. Scan for odd things, check for typosquatting, ensure that dot files do what you expect them to. Well explained piece.
BottomLine: Strong piece on recent AI-code related security issues
